ARM: add CONFIG_ARM_NO_EXCLUSIVES for SoCs without a usable exclusive monitor

The RP2350's exclusive monitor covers SRAM only. LDREX/STREX to any other
region -- including the PSRAM that holds system RAM -- never succeed: measured
0 successes in 1000 operations of 1000 retries each, cached and uncached, while
ordinary loads and stores to the same addresses work perfectly.

This is worse than a fault. RISC-V AMOs on the same silicon *do* fault, which is
why the RISC-V port can trap and emulate them. ARM exclusives simply fail, so a
kernel with its data there does not crash -- it livelocks in the first cmpxchg
retry loop, with no diagnostic at all.

Select this to use interrupt-masked read-modify-write instead, which is what
Linux already does on ARMv5. It is genuinely atomic on a uniprocessor:
interrupts off stops preemption on this core, and !SMP means there is no other.

The !SMP dependency is the entire safety argument, so express it where the build
can enforce it.

--- a/arch/arm/Kconfig-nommu
+++ b/arch/arm/Kconfig-nommu
@@ -64,3 +64,26 @@
 
          If your CPU has an MPU then you should choose 'y' here unless you
          know that you do not want to use the MPU.
+
+config ARM_NO_EXCLUSIVES
+       bool 'Target has no exclusive monitor over all of system RAM'
+       depends on !SMP
+       help
+         Select this on SoCs whose exclusive monitor does not cover the memory
+         used for system RAM.  On the RP2350, for example, LDREX/STREX work
+         only in the internal SRAM and never succeed in the external PSRAM
+         that holds system RAM.
+
+         Such a failure is silent.  STREX reports failure forever rather than
+         faulting, so a kernel using it does not crash -- it livelocks inside
+         the first cmpxchg retry loop with no diagnostic at all.  (RISC-V AMOs
+         on the same silicon do fault, which is why a RISC-V port can trap and
+         emulate them; there is nothing here to trap.)
+
+         Uses interrupt-masked read-modify-write instead, which is what Linux
+         already does on ARMv5.  That is genuinely atomic on a uniprocessor:
+         interrupts off stops preemption on this core, and !SMP means there is
+         no other core to race with.  The !SMP dependency above is the whole of
+         that argument.
+
+         If unsure, say N.
